[{:db/id 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9f013658-0e98-56e3-b0df-e2d26e152fca" "question-9cc85c8f-2fc1-51b5-90b3-b4c40aaa9ac5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133658 :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](images/5b/5b51a8ec772d084cb7c4ac85b6e6ac71e9d128242a6553348e7ea9ec57e8c3dc.svg)" :question/worked-solution "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bccef2e2-efdc-5751-b184-d287795e3e5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133590 :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n![](images/9b/9bb81edc1c3fe4e93de7132c445f1da2ecd20d21c5716068bb0e7c682266bad7.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n![](images/85/85c23bd4ac68777f99fcdef3704db2c3463e33ffd0cd766565e772400337cb37.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=16+16=32\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93aa26c6-5456-5313-9c30-05baafe99b46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133591 :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\n![](images/2e/2e9982bf5d1cd57f39cdc15d8392495e803e4573b09414e551bdca782b76683a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector ![](images/e8/e8a2451004886f6f608537f6a306fd2aceccdab78b934f0111d03e92dcd68834.svg) such that\n\n![](images/16/163b20970f5c0f2a5003cfe8a8f556d7867cecc6f682cb98e2c029bfac8dffb0.svg)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](images/8a/8ab2d8e20b41d277d18616e602e1c7d0cff23ba740258c5fbc0c04f283f8c313.svg)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n![](images/77/779790aeb1fad427347d57850dc157688190eab6c459076a5f04161432ea051c.svg)\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](images/6b/6b6322536d1991a1337709c587681469e578c5163adb9aa5de96eb6d9a89e445.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e056f00-625c-54f9-921d-feb61293e496"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133652 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n![](images/a2/a2681713c4a59f5aaae3aa9a5467d5a06630d3952a8362c1d5e09c9096a28cb1.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$ and\n\n![](images/a0/a0d2a3ef26e93f4f1bca5cb6bdd1ac7fdcd2bcbe8777f5b24717abb808bb29da.svg)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](images/bd/bd0c4759cab77d3ea697fd935e6fe6c3d4e1df2d0b52b9edca3ceb9b4ce48429.svg)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we obtain the vector \n![](images/4e/4eacc3a5c0c299b27178fa31851e73c8da5e9fe4210183f455e4a1880935a98f.svg) \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/ed63dc391add843350216958c99cfdd336a4a87b2a067be9cf29a92faa288cec.svg)\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](images/03/03022ecc797285cd7282c2fd5f6287007ad2d0870a93f825ac5beeadce65df72.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f013658-0e98-56e3-b0df-e2d26e152fca" :question/id #uuid "9f013658-0e98-56e3-b0df-e2d26e152fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-140479" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-3+0-2+2=-3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=4-4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+0-4+2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+6-4-2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+2+4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1c41282-b496-53fd-b601-ecca0a4b4a0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1c41282-b496-53fd-b601-ecca0a4b4a0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c41282-b496-53fd-b601-ecca0a4b4a0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b440af9e-0d7a-5e18-bcec-b34eee928e3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c833776b-5d21-59ee-8ccb-29989a9b9e8a" "answer-7d1dfe6a-46a2-5b7b-b9a2-2e4f0b6a6290" "answer-2e486175-d93e-59a1-beee-b5e17fb3fe9f" "answer-b440af9e-0d7a-5e18-bcec-b34eee928e3f" "answer-99a6a3de-f047-5ffa-a17f-a1fea25b4278"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c833776b-5d21-59ee-8ccb-29989a9b9e8a" :answer/id #uuid "c833776b-5d21-59ee-8ccb-29989a9b9e8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d1dfe6a-46a2-5b7b-b9a2-2e4f0b6a6290" :answer/id #uuid "7d1dfe6a-46a2-5b7b-b9a2-2e4f0b6a6290" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e486175-d93e-59a1-beee-b5e17fb3fe9f" :answer/id #uuid "2e486175-d93e-59a1-beee-b5e17fb3fe9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b440af9e-0d7a-5e18-bcec-b34eee928e3f" :answer/id #uuid "b440af9e-0d7a-5e18-bcec-b34eee928e3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99a6a3de-f047-5ffa-a17f-a1fea25b4278" :answer/id #uuid "99a6a3de-f047-5ffa-a17f-a1fea25b4278" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9cc85c8f-2fc1-51b5-90b3-b4c40aaa9ac5" :question/id #uuid "9cc85c8f-2fc1-51b5-90b3-b4c40aaa9ac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40288" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The set $U$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains  $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most 2 non-zero vectors). Therefore, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=0+10=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=12+3-1=14\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88d26e7d-7cf7-53de-a739-9d6548330c1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88d26e7d-7cf7-53de-a739-9d6548330c1e" :answer-field/id #uuid "88d26e7d-7cf7-53de-a739-9d6548330c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8ea03761-fa6e-5cb3-aed6-0f335e32102b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f442901-c3b7-5866-82d6-1cf54562946a" "answer-902457a9-b694-5355-8804-d4836495776f" "answer-5594b522-b05d-56b3-b912-961b140aeac9" "answer-b4b5b8f0-d7c4-56e8-91b3-1eb6a0e8b98a" "answer-8ea03761-fa6e-5cb3-aed6-0f335e32102b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f442901-c3b7-5866-82d6-1cf54562946a" :answer/id #uuid "8f442901-c3b7-5866-82d6-1cf54562946a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-902457a9-b694-5355-8804-d4836495776f" :answer/id #uuid "902457a9-b694-5355-8804-d4836495776f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5594b522-b05d-56b3-b912-961b140aeac9" :answer/id #uuid "5594b522-b05d-56b3-b912-961b140aeac9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4b5b8f0-d7c4-56e8-91b3-1eb6a0e8b98a" :answer/id #uuid "b4b5b8f0-d7c4-56e8-91b3-1eb6a0e8b98a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea03761-fa6e-5cb3-aed6-0f335e32102b" :answer/id #uuid "8ea03761-fa6e-5cb3-aed6-0f335e32102b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bccef2e2-efdc-5751-b184-d287795e3e5f" :question/id #uuid "bccef2e2-efdc-5751-b184-d287795e3e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82399" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=0+12+6=18\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{12}{25}-\\frac{12}{25}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e09f4fd9-728c-5fad-860e-671199dd6464"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e09f4fd9-728c-5fad-860e-671199dd6464" :answer-field/id #uuid "e09f4fd9-728c-5fad-860e-671199dd6464" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a054e702-a15a-5f24-9814-706df32db073" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2454dddf-8213-5a02-af2b-3a48c11de7c7" "answer-a054e702-a15a-5f24-9814-706df32db073" "answer-1973b4e5-3021-5f0e-84fb-21f789bd603c" "answer-a2e77911-f7b1-5d0a-99ba-fbc6c3057279" "answer-625d32a2-e015-5a3c-a155-566556b8fd99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2454dddf-8213-5a02-af2b-3a48c11de7c7" :answer/id #uuid "2454dddf-8213-5a02-af2b-3a48c11de7c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a054e702-a15a-5f24-9814-706df32db073" :answer/id #uuid "a054e702-a15a-5f24-9814-706df32db073" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1973b4e5-3021-5f0e-84fb-21f789bd603c" :answer/id #uuid "1973b4e5-3021-5f0e-84fb-21f789bd603c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2e77911-f7b1-5d0a-99ba-fbc6c3057279" :answer/id #uuid "a2e77911-f7b1-5d0a-99ba-fbc6c3057279" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-625d32a2-e015-5a3c-a155-566556b8fd99" :answer/id #uuid "625d32a2-e015-5a3c-a155-566556b8fd99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351223 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370894 :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{26}{5} & \\frac{38}{5} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} & ={x}_{3}-2(\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}) \\\\ 5{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{25}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=13$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=-\\frac{5}{19}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-93aa26c6-5456-5313-9c30-05baafe99b46" :question/id #uuid "93aa26c6-5456-5313-9c30-05baafe99b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-81112" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+-2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2(-\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=(-\\frac{3}{5}){x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=10,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-6}{-3}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec92482d-c477-5d24-9a25-7a7af7728403"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec92482d-c477-5d24-9a25-7a7af7728403" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec92482d-c477-5d24-9a25-7a7af7728403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44ed5e4f-8fa9-5c51-90b0-836168dfa8a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-342bd711-7b57-5e5b-b53c-c41f6cf6ed52" "answer-44ed5e4f-8fa9-5c51-90b0-836168dfa8a9" "answer-c083b72f-3913-5a9b-bb4b-2b33c44b8374" "answer-15b18abf-20d4-5b53-b9f6-8b33f580740b" "answer-8e2ec803-638b-5ea2-ac14-1aac19cbda75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-342bd711-7b57-5e5b-b53c-c41f6cf6ed52" :answer/id #uuid "342bd711-7b57-5e5b-b53c-c41f6cf6ed52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44ed5e4f-8fa9-5c51-90b0-836168dfa8a9" :answer/id #uuid "44ed5e4f-8fa9-5c51-90b0-836168dfa8a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c083b72f-3913-5a9b-bb4b-2b33c44b8374" :answer/id #uuid "c083b72f-3913-5a9b-bb4b-2b33c44b8374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15b18abf-20d4-5b53-b9f6-8b33f580740b" :answer/id #uuid "15b18abf-20d4-5b53-b9f6-8b33f580740b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e2ec803-638b-5ea2-ac14-1aac19cbda75" :answer/id #uuid "8e2ec803-638b-5ea2-ac14-1aac19cbda75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e056f00-625c-54f9-921d-feb61293e496" :question/id #uuid "9e056f00-625c-54f9-921d-feb61293e496" :question/math-academy-id "q-83768" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{21}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{14}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}|=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{14}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6bbf667-f0ec-5670-9c25-934e766d8f09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6bbf667-f0ec-5670-9c25-934e766d8f09" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bbf667-f0ec-5670-9c25-934e766d8f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f906b041-d06e-5690-ab61-f1fbb040be89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2644191-8b0e-5649-af0f-0f586b12d76d" "answer-ab5a79ce-b244-589b-887d-eb3fa120294b" "answer-25cb96ba-aaf0-5535-b2bf-14442cb3e2c8" "answer-93c0c141-6245-5b52-b5d1-782e45040c14" "answer-f906b041-d06e-5690-ab61-f1fbb040be89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2644191-8b0e-5649-af0f-0f586b12d76d" :answer/id #uuid "c2644191-8b0e-5649-af0f-0f586b12d76d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab5a79ce-b244-589b-887d-eb3fa120294b" :answer/id #uuid "ab5a79ce-b244-589b-887d-eb3fa120294b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25cb96ba-aaf0-5535-b2bf-14442cb3e2c8" :answer/id #uuid "25cb96ba-aaf0-5535-b2bf-14442cb3e2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93c0c141-6245-5b52-b5d1-782e45040c14" :answer/id #uuid "93c0c141-6245-5b52-b5d1-782e45040c14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f906b041-d06e-5690-ab61-f1fbb040be89" :answer/id #uuid "f906b041-d06e-5690-ab61-f1fbb040be89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351226 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135045 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n![](images/9b/9b5afbbe850cb936b16b177e0a1b42bdd94df9bdc5dd3830aadc0de328a2b0d8.svg)\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133990 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
